 - [`Recompiler.cpp`](../../core/shader/recompiler/Recompiler.cpp) runs the stages in this order. With `ANYPS5_ENABLE_SPIRV_TOOLS`, the SPIR-V is also validated and optimized with SPIRV-Tools.
-- `ShaderRecompiler::Recompile` keeps compiled variants in memory, and `ShaderDiskCache` stores them on disk so later runs reuse them. Before the writable cache, a miss is also looked up in read-only prebuilt packs and loose entry folders under `shader_cache/prebuilt`; pack entries are ordinary encoded cache entries behind a sorted key-hash index, validated by magic, compiler source version and index checksum when mapped, and a miss or rejected entry falls through to the writable cache and the compiler. The cache format (`ShaderDiskCache::FormatVersion`) stores, among other things, the finite stencil-reference set, its replay-safety proof and reserved push-data word in the artifact and binding layouts. An entry of any other format, or written by another compiler source version, is rejected.
+- `ShaderRecompiler::Recompile` keeps compiled variants in memory, and `ShaderDiskCache` stores them on disk so later runs reuse them. Before the writable cache, a miss is also looked up in read-only prebuilt packs and loose entry folders under `shader_cache/prebuilt`; pack entries are ordinary encoded cache entries behind a sorted key-hash index, validated by magic, compiler source version and index checksum when mapped, and a miss or rejected entry falls through to the writable cache and the compiler. The cache format (`ShaderDiskCache::FormatVersion`) stores, among other things, the finite stencil-reference set, its replay-safety proof and reserved push-data word in the artifact and binding layouts. An entry of any other format, or written by another compiler source version, is rejected. A resource plan build or variant emission that throws (the failures `Recompile` remembers in memory) is stored as well, under the same key, as its error text and the code address it failed at; a later run rethrows it at that address without compiling. `APS5_NO_FAILURE_MEMO=1` neither stores nor reads them.
 - The Vulkan pipelines are journaled as well ([`PipelineJournal.cpp`](../../core/libs/prx/libSceAgcDriver/Graphics/src/PipelineJournal.cpp)): a graphics pipeline the store builds (`CachedPipeline`) or a compute pipeline a dispatch builds is appended to `pipelines.journal` next to the shader cache entries, as the materialized result of each stage (the specialized SPIR-V, bindings and push constants, through `ShaderDiskCache::EncodeResult`) plus the recipe the pipeline was built from (`GraphicsPipelineRecipe`: the stages' identities, vertex input layout, descriptor set layout keys and the state fields the `Pipeline` constructor reads, `CanonicalPipelineState`). When a `VulkanDevice` is created, the journal is loaded and its pipelines are built on a pool of `hardware_concurrency() / 2` threads (`APS5_PIPELINE_JOURNAL_THREADS`) while the title loads. The load parses the file a record at a time and keeps it open, and a replay thread reads an entry's modules from it when it builds that entry (a record that no longer reads back as loaded rejects the entry), so the replay holds the modules of the entries being built rather than the file, so the first draw or dispatch of a configuration seen on an earlier run finds its pipeline resident. The pipeline stores key on `RecompileResult::Identity`, a hash of the module words, specialization constants, subgroup width and bindings that is the same on every run (`SharedSpirv::ContentHash`, computed once per shared module), rather than on the process-lifetime variant ids; compute pipelines and their journal modules key on `DescribedPipelineIdentity` instead, the same hash with the element count of image, storage image and sampler table bindings left out, since `DescribeSetLayouts` gives those tables a fixed capacity and two results that differ only in a table's size build the same pipeline. A graphics pipeline is built outside the store's mutex, so the replay threads and the queue workers build in parallel. A draw that arrives before its replayed entry builds the pipeline itself; the first inserted wins. A replayed entry enters the store at its least recently used end and a full store takes none, so the title's own pipelines are never evicted for a replayed one, and the optimized relink of a replayed pipeline (`OptimizedPipeline::Schedule`) waits for the first draw that binds it, so the relink thread serves the title's pipelines first. A device created without a window starts its replay at its first draw or dispatch rather than at creation, since `Driver::AttachWindow` usually replaces it before the title draws. The journal records a pipeline once (modules and recipes are deduplicated by content), keeps at most `APS5_PIPELINE_JOURNAL_MAX_MIB` (1024), replays the last `APS5_PIPELINE_JOURNAL_ENTRIES` recorded (4096, the pipeline store's largest bound: a replayed pipeline that finds the store full grows its bound from 1024 instead of being dropped), and is disabled with `APS5_NO_PIPELINE_JOURNAL=1` or without a shader cache directory. The file header names the device (`PipelineJournalDeviceFingerprint`, the properties the driver's pipeline cache file is named by), so a journal written for another GPU or driver is started over rather than replayed; appends and reads hold `pipelines.journal.lock` against another process sharing the directory, and a device cancels its replay before it is replaced (`PrepareForReplacement`) or destroyed, so a replaced headless device never inserts entries the window's device then sweeps. `APS5_PROFILE_DRAW=1` prints a `[pipeline-journal]` summary when the replay finishes and at teardown.
 - A compute shader whose LDS plus its lock dword exceeds the host's `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` is not given a `Workgroup` array. `Recompile` sets `ShaderInfo::sharedMemoryBytes` for it and the SPIR-V backend addresses the LDS in a coherent storage buffer at descriptor set 1 (`WorkgroupMemoryDescriptorSet`), with a slice of `RecompileResult::workgroupMemoryDwords` per workgroup, indexed by `WorkgroupID` and `NumWorkGroups`. `VulkanDevice` keeps one such buffer, sized from the dispatch dimensions, and binds it after the resource set; programs that fit keep using shared memory.
 - `Driver::RegisterShader` splits shader preparation in two. `PlanRegistered` runs on the registering guest thread: it validates the registers and entry point, decodes the code and builds the recompile request, so invalid registrations still fail there. The RDNA to IR translation and resource plan (`PrepareShader`) run on a background pool of `hardware_concurrency() / 2` threads (at least 2, 64 MiB stacks), which `Driver::stop` joins when it closes the pool.
